  "id": "Compound::DB00321 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Amitriptyline is implicated in the regulation of Chrm2 through a series of interconnected pathways that involve the modulation of taste perception and the binding of neurotransmitters to the receptor. The evidence suggests that Amitriptyline, through its role in causing Taste Peculiar, indirectly influences Chrm2 via the binding of related compounds such as Imipramine, Nortriptyline, and Doxepin. These compounds, all belonging to the tricyclic class of antidepressants, share a common mechanism of action involving the interaction with Chrm2. The lowest common ancestors of Amitriptyline and its related compounds—Tricyclic Antidepressant and Organic Tricyclic Compound—highlight a shared pharmacological profile that may contribute to the observed effects on Chrm2. The integration of these pathways underscores a potential mechanism by which Amitriptyline, through its interaction with related compounds, modulates Chrm2, likely through the activation of receptor signaling pathways. The evidence supports a relationship between Amitriptyline and Chrm2, mediated by the binding of related compounds that share a common structural and functional profile.",
